::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon="false"}
## Learning objectives

By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

- compute raw, standardized, studentized, and jackknife residuals and say what each is for;
- read residual, Q-Q, scale-location, and leverage plots to check the LINE assumptions;
- try a transformation of the outcome and judge whether it helped;
- find outliers with a Bonferroni-corrected jackknife test, and influential points with leverage, Cook's distance, DFFITS, and DFBETAS;
- measure collinearity with the variance inflation factor and reduce structural collinearity by centering;
- recognize the classic diagnostic patterns in simulated examples.
:::

## Introduction

After fitting a regression model, we must ask: **does the model actually fit the data well, and are the underlying assumptions satisfied?** A model that violates key assumptions may produce misleading coefficients, invalid standard errors, or unreliable predictions.

Regression diagnostics address three fundamental questions:

1. **Are the regression assumptions satisfied?** (Linearity, Independence, Normality, Equal Variance)
2. **Is there multicollinearity among predictors?** (Inflated standard errors, unstable coefficients)
3. **Are there influential observations?** (Outliers, high-leverage points that distort the fitted model)

This lecture provides the tools to diagnose each of these issues and, where possible, apply remedial measures. Diagnostics should be a routine part of every regression analysis, not an afterthought.

## Setup and Data


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(tidyverse)
library(brfssdata)
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)
library(broom)
library(gtsummary)
library(car)
```
:::


### The BRFSS 2020 Dataset

We continue with the BRFSS 2020 dataset. For this lecture, we predict **mentally unhealthy days** from several health-related variables.

::: {.column-margin}
BRFSS 2020 codebook: [CDC annual data page](https://www.cdc.gov/brfss/annual_data/annual_2020.html). Package documentation: [brfssdata](https://muntasirmasum.github.io/brfssdata/).
:::

::: {.data-card}
[Data for this lecture]{.eyebrow}

**BRFSS 2020, loaded with the brfssdata R package.** The first time you run the code below, `read_brfss()` downloads the 2020 survey (about 21 MB) and keeps it on your computer, so later runs read it from there. We then recode eight variables and draw a random sample of 5,000 adults with complete data.

Install the packages once with `install.packages(c("brfssdata", "janitor"))`.
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Read the eight BRFSS 2020 variables this lecture uses (CDC names;
# names that start with an underscore need quotes)
brfss_full <- read_brfss(
  2020,
  vars = c("MENTHLTH", "PHYSHLTH", "SLEPTIM1", "_AGE80", "SEXVAR", "_BMI5",
           "EXERANY2", "INCOME2")
) |>
  janitor::clean_names()
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
brfss_dx <- brfss_full |>
  mutate(
    # Outcome
    menthlth_days = case_when(
      menthlth == 88                  ~ 0,
      menthlth >= 1 & menthlth <= 30 ~ as.numeric(menthlth),
      TRUE                           ~ NA_real_
    ),
    # Predictors
    physhlth_days = case_when(
      physhlth == 88                  ~ 0,
      physhlth >= 1 & physhlth <= 30 ~ as.numeric(physhlth),
      TRUE                           ~ NA_real_
    ),
    sleep_hrs = case_when(
      sleptim1 >= 1 & sleptim1 <= 14 ~ as.numeric(sleptim1),
      TRUE                           ~ NA_real_
    ),
    age = age80,
    sex = factor(sexvar, levels = c(1, 2), labels = c("Male", "Female")),
    bmi = ifelse(bmi5 > 0, bmi5 / 100, NA_real_),
    exercise = factor(case_when(
      exerany2 == 1 ~ "Yes",
      exerany2 == 2 ~ "No",
      TRUE          ~ NA_character_
    ), levels = c("No", "Yes")),
    income_cat = case_when(
      income2 %in% 1:8 ~ as.numeric(income2),
      TRUE             ~ NA_real_
    )
  ) |>
  filter(
    !is.na(menthlth_days), !is.na(physhlth_days), !is.na(sleep_hrs),
    !is.na(age), age >= 18, !is.na(sex), !is.na(bmi),
    !is.na(exercise), !is.na(income_cat)
  )

set.seed(1220)
brfss_dx <- brfss_dx |>
  dplyr::select(menthlth_days, physhlth_days, sleep_hrs, age, sex,
                bmi, exercise, income_cat) |>
  slice_sample(n = 5000)

tibble(Metric = c("Observations", "Variables"),
       Value  = c(nrow(brfss_dx), ncol(brfss_dx))) |>
  kable(caption = "Analytic Dataset Dimensions") |>
  kable_styling(bootstrap_options = "striped", full_width = FALSE)
```

